  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nMain weather story this week will be the late season heat wave,\nwith afternoon heat indices reaching the upper 90s to around 100\ntoday and Friday and 100-105 Tuesday through Thursday. We\nexpanded the heat advisory ne to Stark, Peoria, Tazewell, Logan,\nDeWitt, Piatt, Douglas, Coles and Clark counties from this\nafternoon through Thu afternoon. We may eventually need to \nexpand the heat advisory further ne over the remainder of CWA \n(Marshall, Woodford, McLean, Champaign, Edgar and Vermilion \ncounties) from Tuesday through Thursday.\n\nThe 08Z/3 am surface analysis shows 1020-1022 mb high pressure \nover the Appalachians and mid Atlantic States. Meanwhile a\nfrontal boundary extended from near the MN and Dakotas border\ninto central Nebraska. A light SSE flow of warm and rather humid\nair mass was over IL. 3 am temps were in the upper 60s to mid \n70s while dewpoints were in the low to mid 60s. Patches of\nmid/high clouds were in eastern IL and from Galesburg to Decatur\nne and more widespread clouds in ne and northern IL where\nscattered showers were from Lacon to Hoopeston northeast and\ntracking ESE. A 30-45 kt sw low level jet extended from central\nKS ne over eastern Iowa, central/se WI and nw IL and helping\nsupport these showers. As sw low level jet wanes during the\nmorning, these showers and isolated thunderstorms north of I-74\nshould diminish. Strong 500 mb high pressure ridge over the mid\nMS river valley to build northward with 500 mb heights rising to\nor above 594 decameters over CWA. 850 mb temps to rise to 22-24C\nthis afternoon and warmest over western CWA. Skies to become \npartly to mostly sunny today over central/se IL and SSW winds \n10-15 mph and gusts to around 20 mph to bring in the heat with\nhighs 90-96F (warmest south of I-70 and from Springfield sw).\nHeat indices this afternoon peak in the upper 90s to around\n100F, warmest readings near or just above 100F over western CWA\nand from highway 50 south in southeast IL. \n\nNocturnal low level jet to our north/nw tonight could spark some\nconvection overnight into Tue morning but should stay along and north\nof I-80. GFS model is an outlying with convection getting as far\nsouth as I-72 late tonight and Tue morning. The strong \nmid/upper level ridge stays near IL Tue/Wed with temperatures \ngetting a bit higher into the 90s over CWA and afternoon heat \nindices 100-105F (peaking around Wed). Will likely need to \nexpand heat advisory further ne over rest of CWA during mid\nweek. Mid/upper level ridge flattens on Thu and could see 20-30%\nchance of convection getting into northern/ne CWA by Thu\nmorning. Highs Thu in the lower 90s ne CWA and mid to upper 90s\nsw CWA with afternoon heat indices 100-105F. \n\nForecast models continue to show 500 mb high pressure ridge\nretrograding westward into the Central Plains and MO valley late\nthis week and this weekend with IL getting into a nw and\npossibly more unsettled flow. NBM has slight chances of\nconvection over much of CWA overnight Thu night and Fri and\nagain overnight Fri night and Sat. May see higher chances of\nconvection during this time frame but too difficult to pin down\nin this pattern that far out. Does look like the heat wave eases\nslightly late this week into the weekend with more clouds\nexpected and possible convection chances. Highs Fri in the lower\n90s with afternoon heat indices in the upper 90s to near 100F.\nHighs of 85-90 on Sunday/Monday with dry weather expected as\nupper level heights rise again over IL with ridging from the \nCentral Plains.",
